REV.page({"course":"english1","id":"w10","title":"Common errors and writing basics","summary":"Two lectures on errors that even educated users make (uncountable nouns, verbs that take no -ing or -ed, and subject–verb agreement with the head of a noun phrase), then four on writing: what makes writing good, and how to write a bio-note, describe a photograph and write about a public problem, always by drafting, cutting and revising.","n":10,"lectures":[{"code":"10.1","title":"Common errors in english | part 2 | with grammar tips for better fluency","video":"Z8L7vIbAVK0","seconds":1944},{"code":"10.2","title":"Common errors in english | part 1 | with subject verb agreement tips","video":"LNczGDRKX6Y","seconds":1505},{"code":"10.3","title":"Basics of writing | grammar, structure & style for effective communication","video":"EJN3-yzAhqU","seconds":1473},{"code":"10.4","title":"How to write bio-notes? | concise & impactful self-introduction tips","video":"5JkSz17l1Ao","seconds":2283},{"code":"10.5","title":"Describe a photo | techniques for clear & concise visual description","video":"WMjTEHW6YsU","seconds":1981},{"code":"10.6","title":"Writing for public purpose| clarity, brevity & impact in civic issue reports","video":"AF8akyH8Duo","seconds":1978}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"common-errors-1-nouns-and-verbs-that-break-the-pattern","level":2,"title":"Common errors 1: nouns and verbs that break the pattern"},{"id":"uncountable-nouns","level":3,"title":"Uncountable nouns"},{"id":"why-can-t-we-say-breads","level":3,"title":"Why can’t we say “breads”?"},{"id":"talking-about-amounts","level":3,"title":"Talking about amounts"},{"id":"verbs-that-do-not-take-ing","level":3,"title":"Verbs that do not take -ing"},{"id":"verbs-with-one-form","level":3,"title":"Verbs with one form"},{"id":"common-errors-2-subject-verb-agreement-and-the-head","level":2,"title":"Common errors 2: subject–verb agreement and the head"},{"id":"how-to-find-the-head","level":3,"title":"How to find the head"},{"id":"agreement-in-a-story","level":3,"title":"Agreement in a story"},{"id":"what-makes-writing-good","level":2,"title":"What makes writing good"},{"id":"a-short-history-of-writing","level":3,"title":"A short history of writing"},{"id":"why-writing-matters","level":3,"title":"Why writing matters"},{"id":"the-basics-of-good-writing","level":3,"title":"The basics of good writing"},{"id":"bio-notes","level":2,"title":"Bio-notes"},{"id":"mission-and-vision-statements","level":3,"title":"Mission and vision statements"},{"id":"choosing-what-to-say","level":3,"title":"Choosing what to say"},{"id":"two-drafts-of-one-bio-note","level":3,"title":"Two drafts of one bio-note"},{"id":"a-senior-academic-s-bio-note","level":3,"title":"A senior academic’s bio-note"},{"id":"describing-a-photograph","level":2,"title":"Describing a photograph"},{"id":"look-before-you-write","level":3,"title":"Look before you write"},{"id":"two-drafts","level":3,"title":"Two drafts"},{"id":"what-the-final-draft-removed","level":3,"title":"What the final draft removed"},{"id":"accurate-words","level":3,"title":"Accurate words"},{"id":"writing-for-public-purposes","level":2,"title":"Writing for public purposes"},{"id":"civic-problems","level":3,"title":"Civic problems"},{"id":"planning-a-letter-on-waterlogging","level":3,"title":"Planning a letter on waterlogging"},{"id":"a-shop-notice","level":3,"title":"A shop notice"},{"id":"two-drafts-of-the-letter","level":3,"title":"Two drafts of the letter"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Uncountable nouns</strong> (10.1): <em>information, equipment, furniture, milk, rice, bread, water …</em> take no <em>a/an</em> and no <em>-s/-es</em>. The dictionary marks them <strong>[U]</strong>. To show an amount, use a <strong>partitive</strong>: <em>some information, a loaf of bread, a litre of water</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Verbs that break the pattern</strong> (10.1): in the lecture’s rule, <em>appear, feel, have, look, seem, stand</em> are not used with <em>-ing</em> (✓ <em>It seems correct</em>, ✗ <em>It is seeming correct</em>), except in other meanings: <em>having breakfast</em> (eat), <em>standing for election</em> (contest), <em>feeling the pulse</em> (touch). Standard English is less strict with <em>feel</em>, <em>look</em> and <em>stand</em>: <em>I’m feeling fine</em> and <em>A policeman is standing at the post</em> are correct. <em>Cast, cost, cut, put</em> have the same form in all tenses: ✗ <em>casted</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Subject–verb agreement</strong> (10.2): the verb agrees with the <strong>head</strong> of the noun phrase, not with the noun nearest to it: <em>Some <strong>boys</strong> of Rajendra Hostel <strong>are</strong> making all the noise.</em> In noun + noun the <strong>second</strong> noun is the head (<em>history <strong>teacher</strong></em>); in noun + preposition + noun the noun <strong>before</strong> the preposition is the head (<em><strong>inspector</strong> of police</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Good writing</strong> (10.3): writing <strong>lasts long and goes far</strong>. The most important thing is <strong>content</strong>; then <strong>small words and simple sentences</strong>; writing is a <strong>skill</strong> learnt by daily practice; a good script comes after <strong>several drafts</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Bio-notes</strong> (10.4): a short note (often 50 to 200 words) on <strong>what sort of person you are</strong> and what makes you <strong>different</strong>. No clichés, no redundant words, accurate words. The lecture’s sample went from 278 words in 25 sentences to 155 words in 13.</li>\n<li><strong>Describing a photograph</strong> (10.5): look at everything first, then describe <strong>facts, not attitudes</strong>, inside the word limit (150 words means about 145 to 155). The sample went from 192 words in 13 sentences to 146 words in 14.</li>\n<li><strong>Writing for public purposes</strong> (10.6): be <strong>formal, factual, precise and brief</strong>. A civic problem is a <strong>man-made</strong> problem. Gather facts, describe the harm, suggest remedies. The sample letter went from 291 words with little information to 148 words with a lot.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All six lectures are by Prof. Shreesh Chaudhary.</p>\n<h2 id=\"common-errors-1-nouns-and-verbs-that-break-the-pattern\">Common errors 1: nouns and verbs that break the pattern</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 10.1 looks at “some errors frequently made by students learning English”. It corrects them, shows <strong>why</strong> they happen, and helps us make sure “that they do not recur” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). Even “certified users” of English make these mistakes, which is why they are called <strong>common errors</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>).</p>\n<p>Why do they happen? Lecture 10.2 gives the reason: <strong>over-generalisation</strong>. We learn a pattern and use it everywhere, even where English does not use it: “we say <em>eating</em>, so why can’t we say <em>seeming</em> or <em>looking</em> or <em>feeling</em>?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>). Most words follow the pattern; a few words “behave differently”, and we must learn those few (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Part 1 and Part 2</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slides call Lecture 10.1 “Common errors in English, <strong>Part 1</strong>” and Lecture 10.2 “<strong>Part 2</strong>”. The YouTube titles have the two numbers the other way round. This page follows the slides and the course order.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"uncountable-nouns\">Uncountable nouns</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">One wrong sentence (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>✗ <em>We got some informations.</em> The slide calls it ungrammatical (wrong by the rules of grammar).</li>\n<li><em>Information</em> has no plural. It stays in the form the dictionary gives.</li>\n<li>So you can say: ✓ <em>I want some information.</em> ✓ <em>I can give some information.</em> ✓ <em>for further information</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> ✓ <em>We got some information.</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Countable and uncountable nouns</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>countable noun</strong> names something we can count one by one: <em>an apple, two apples</em>. It takes <em>a/an</em> in the singular and <em>-s/-es</em> in the plural.</li>\n<li>An <strong>uncountable noun</strong> (also called a <strong>mass noun</strong>) names something we do not count one by one: <em>water, rice, information</em>. It takes <strong>no</strong> <em>a/an</em> and has <strong>no</strong> plural form (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The slide: “Some nouns have no plural; you use them only in the singular number as given in the dictionary. So, for instance, words like <strong>equipment, meat, oil, rice, wheat, milk, information, fish, furniture</strong> do not have a singular or plural form” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=545s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:05</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Citation form</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The form of a word as it is <strong>cited</strong> (given) in the dictionary: <em>information</em>, <em>furniture</em>. An uncountable noun is always used in its citation form, never with <em>-s</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">What the dictionary tells you (the lecture’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture shows a dictionary’s page for <em>information</em>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Part of speech:</strong> <em>noun</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>[U]</strong>, right after it. Every dictionary lists its abbreviations at the beginning, and there <strong>U</strong> means <strong>uncountable</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). The opposite is <strong>C</strong>, countable. If a noun shows no letter at all, the lecturer says, it is countable; an uncountable noun is always marked (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Pronunciation:</strong> a sound button, and the word in phonetic symbols (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Meaning:</strong> “facts about a situation, person, event, etc.” Facts that can be checked (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Use in sentences</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>):\n<ul>\n<li><em>Do you have any information <strong>about/on</strong> train times?</em></li>\n<li><em>I read an interesting <strong>bit/piece</strong> of information in the newspaper.</em> (The <em>an</em> goes with <em>bit</em> or <em>piece</em>, not with <em>information</em>.)</li>\n<li><em><strong>For further</strong> information (= if you want to know more), please contact your local library.</em></li>\n<li><em>We have reliable information <strong>that</strong> a strike is planned next month.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> a good dictionary gives six or seven kinds of information about a word. So say “six or seven <strong>kinds of</strong> information”, never ✗ “six or seven informations” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Milk as a noun and as a verb (the lecture’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=682s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:22</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>When <em>milk</em> is a <strong>noun</strong>, you cannot add <em>-s</em>: ✗ <em>I like cow and buffalo milks.</em></li>\n<li>“You can only say”: ✓ <em>I like both cow milk and buffalo milk.</em></li>\n<li>In the same way: ✓ <em>I take apples for breakfast</em>, but ✗ <em>I take milks for breakfast</em>. ✓ <em>I take goat milk for breakfast</em>, “like Gandhiji used to” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Milks</em> is possible only when <em>milk</em> is a <strong>verb</strong> (to take milk from an animal): ✓ <em>Farmers milk the cows.</em> ✓ <em>A farmer milks his cow</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Which words take a/an or a plural? (the lecture’s Activity 1, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=739s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:19</a> and the answers at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=865s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:25</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The words: <em>apple, bread, call, egg, meat, petrol, rice, school, sugar, tea, tiger, umbrella, water, wheat, year, zebra</em>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Countable</th>\n<th>With a/an</th>\n<th>Plural</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>apple</td>\n<td>an apple</td>\n<td>apples</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>call</td>\n<td>a call</td>\n<td>calls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>egg</td>\n<td>an egg</td>\n<td>eggs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>school</td>\n<td>a school</td>\n<td>schools</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>tiger</td>\n<td>a tiger</td>\n<td>tigers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>umbrella</td>\n<td>an umbrella</td>\n<td>umbrellas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>year</td>\n<td>a year</td>\n<td>years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>zebra</td>\n<td>a zebra</td>\n<td>zebras</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Words with no plural form and no a/an</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=941s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:41</a>): <em>bread, petrol, rice, sugar, tea, water, wheat, meat</em>. So ✗ <em>breads, petrols, rices, sugars, teas, waters</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>). (That holds for the food or drink itself. A few of these words also have a countable use for a cup, a spoonful or a kind, and dictionaries show it: <em>Two teas, please</em>; <em>How many sugars?</em>; <em>the breads of India</em>. This note is mine.)</p>\n<p>The lecture’s sentences: <em>Raghavachari has <strong>an apple</strong> for breakfast every morning.</em> <em>Her husband gets <strong>calls</strong> until midnight.</em> <em>Ismail has <strong>an egg</strong> every day for breakfast.</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">A or an?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>An apple, an egg, an umbrella</em> but <em>a year, a zebra</em>: the choice depends on the first <strong>sound</strong> of the next word, as in <a href=\"#/english1/w02/articles-a-and-an\">Week 2</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Fish and furniture</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide lists <em>fish</em> with the uncountable nouns, in the sense of food: ✓ <em>You ate fish. You ate a lot of fish</em>, not ✗ <em>I ate two fishes</em>. The lecturer allows <em>fishes</em> only “perhaps in rare case”, for a <strong>variety</strong> of fish (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>). Similarly, ✗ <em>I have bought furnitures</em>; say ✓ <em>I bought several pieces of furniture</em> or name the things: <em>a table, a chair, some bookshelves</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</p>\n<p>Two cautions (mine). As an animal, <em>fish</em> is countable, and its plural is usually <em>fish</em>: <em>I caught two fish</em>; <em>The cat ate a fish</em>. And <em>head</em> counts cattle (<em>fifty head of cattle</em>), not fish: for one portion, everyday English says <em>a piece of fish</em>, not the slide’s <em>a head of fish</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"why-can-t-we-say-breads\">Why can’t we say “breads”?</h3>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“You can ask why <em>bread</em> cannot be counted. In Hindi, it is a countable noun. These things are not always logical, nor are they alike in all languages” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1004s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:44</a>). <em>Breads</em> is easy to say, and in Hindi the word for bread, <em>roti</em>, has a plural. But “language is not always mathematical, language is not always logical” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</p>\n<p>The lecturer compares it with <strong>grammatical gender</strong>. Hindi gives every noun a gender, masculine or feminine, even to things such as a table or a chair, and “there is no logic behind” which one a word gets. Sanskrit has three genders (masculine, feminine, neuter) and French has two (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>). Modern English has no masculine or feminine nouns, but it does sort nouns into <strong>countable</strong> and <strong>uncountable</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>). Both sortings are <strong>arbitrary</strong> (fixed by custom, not by reason). So “we must learn that some nouns cannot be used in English with an <em>a/an</em> or <em>-s/es</em>.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"talking-about-amounts\">Talking about amounts</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Quantifiers and partitives</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An uncountable noun cannot take <em>a</em> or a number. To show “a little of it” or “a lot of it”, English uses other words before it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a <strong>quantifier</strong>, a word that shows a quantity (amount): <em>some, a little</em>;</li>\n<li>a <strong>partitive</strong> (the lecturer also says “partitive adjective”), a phrase that names a <strong>part</strong> or a <strong>unit</strong>: <em>a piece of, a loaf of, a litre of</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1224s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:24</a></th>\n<th>Notes from the lecture</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>some information</td>\n<td>also <em>some oil, some sugar, some rice, some meat</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a little knowledge</td>\n<td><em>a little information</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a news item / all the news</td>\n<td>✗ <em>a news</em>; ✓ <em>I have some news for you</em>, <em>good news, bad news, exciting news</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a loaf / slice / morsel of bread</td>\n<td>plural <em>loaves of bread, slices of bread</em>; <em>They haven’t had a morsel of bread</em> (a very small piece)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a head of fish</td>\n<td>✗ <em>I eat a fish</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>); see the cautions in the note on fish above</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a portion of meat</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a gallon of petrol</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a grain of rice / wheat</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a litre of water</td>\n<td>also oil, or any other liquid</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Liquids and solids</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A liquid is measured in <strong>litres</strong> or <strong>gallons</strong>; a solid in <strong>kilograms</strong>, <strong>quintals</strong> or <strong>tons</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>). When you meet a new uncountable noun, also note the unit that goes with it.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Activity 2 (the lecture’s, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1376s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:56</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Look up your dictionary and (a) find at least a dozen other uncountable nouns, which do not take <em>a/an</em> or <em>-s/es</em>; (b) note how small or large quantities of them are mentioned. Prefer <strong>frequent</strong> words: “it is better to check if <em>cat</em> is plural than to check if <em>zebra</em> is plural” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>). To start you off (mine): <em>luggage</em> (<em>a piece of luggage</em>), <em>homework</em> (<em>a lot of homework</em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"verbs-that-do-not-take-ing\">Verbs that do not take -ing</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The progressive (continuous) form</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Be</em> + verb + <em>-ing</em>: <em>I <strong>am eating</strong></em>, <em>she <strong>was writing</strong></em>. It shows an action in progress (see <a href=\"#/english1/w05/present-progressive\">Week 5</a>).</p>\n<p>The slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1415s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:35</a>: “Some verbs, such as the following, are not used in progressive mode, with + ing: <strong>appear, feel, have, look, seem, stand</strong>, etc. You can use this verb in the simple present or past tense form, but not in the progressive present or past tense form.”</p>\n<p>(Grammar books call such verbs <strong>stative verbs</strong>: they describe a <strong>state</strong>, not an action.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>✗ Cannot say</th>\n<th>✓ Can say</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>You are appearing to be tired.</td>\n<td>You appear to be tired.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I am feeling all right.</td>\n<td>I feel all right.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I am having a car.</td>\n<td>I have a car.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>You are looking angry.</td>\n<td>You look angry.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>It is seeming correct.</td>\n<td>It seems correct.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A policeman is standing at the post.</td>\n<td>A policeman stands at the post.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(The table on the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1491s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:51</a>.) The same holds in the past: ✓ <em>It felt all right</em>, ✗ <em>I was feeling all right</em> (by the lecture’s rule; see the note below); ✓ <em>She looked angry</em>, ✓ <em>She looked tired</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">When the same verbs can take -ing</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>With <strong>another meaning</strong>, these verbs can be progressive (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1527s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:27</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Verb</th>\n<th>New meaning</th>\n<th>The lecture’s sentence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>have</td>\n<td>eat</td>\n<td><em>I am having breakfast.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>stand</td>\n<td>contest (try to win an election)</td>\n<td><em>Gopal is standing for election to the parliament.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>feel</td>\n<td>touch to examine</td>\n<td><em>I am feeling the patient’s pulse</em> (said by a doctor).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Otherwise “you don’t say <em>-ing</em> with <em>feel, look, seem, appear</em>” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Dictionaries are a little less strict</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide also marks ✗ <em>I am having a meeting</em> (✓ <em>I have a meeting already</em>). Many dictionaries accept <em>have</em> + <em>-ing</em> when <em>have</em> means “hold” or “take part in” an event (<em>We are having a meeting now</em>), just like <em>having breakfast</em>. Grammar books also accept <em>feel</em> and <em>look</em> with <em>-ing</em> for how someone feels or looks just now (<em>I’m feeling fine</em>, <em>You’re looking tired</em>), and <em>stand</em> with <em>-ing</em> for where a person is standing at this moment: <em>A policeman is standing at the post</em> is correct (<em>stands</em> says where he stands as a rule). The lecture’s ✗ marks are safe for <em>have</em> in the sense of <strong>own</strong> (<em>I am having a car</em>), for <em>seem</em> and for <em>appear</em>. In this course, know the lecture’s rule; in your own English, these other forms are not errors.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Activity 3 (the lecture’s, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1614s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:54</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Check your dictionary and make a list of verbs that cannot be used in the progressive mode. You can also check for exceptions.” Two to start with (mine): ✗ <em>I am knowing him</em>, ✓ <em>I know him</em>; ✗ <em>This bag is belonging to me</em>, ✓ <em>This bag belongs to me</em>. “No matter what we do, we cannot do everything in a classroom,” so “rather than learn everything, it is better that we should learn <strong>how to learn</strong> everything” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"verbs-with-one-form\">Verbs with one form</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Same form in all tenses</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Some verbs, such as the following, have the same form in all tenses: <strong>cast, cost, cut, put</strong>” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1640s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:20</a>). Their past tense and past participle are the same as the base form: ✗ <em>casted, costed, cutted, putted</em>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Base form</th>\n<th>Past</th>\n<th>Past participle</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>cast</td>\n<td>cast</td>\n<td>cast</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cost</td>\n<td>cost</td>\n<td>cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cut</td>\n<td>cut</td>\n<td>cut</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>put</td>\n<td>put</td>\n<td>put</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1752s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:12</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>I <strong>cast</strong> my vote.</em></li>\n<li><em>This book <strong>cost</strong> me 500 rupees.</em></li>\n<li><em>Before we go to the kitchen, the apple <strong>is cut</strong>.</em></li>\n<li><em>I am almost sure that I <strong>put</strong> my phone here yesterday.</em></li>\n<li>✓ <em>Votes have been <strong>cast</strong>, result is awaited.</em> ✗ <em>Votes have been casted, result is awaited.</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p>“You can use these verbs only in the given form in all situations, because they have no other form” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1734s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:54</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">They still take -s and -ing</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“No other form” is about the <strong>past</strong>: no <em>-ed</em>. In the present they still take <em>-s</em> with <em>he, she, it</em>, like any verb: <em>This book <strong>costs</strong> 500 rupees today</em>; <em>He <strong>puts</strong> sugar in his tea</em>; <em>She is <strong>cutting</strong> the apple</em> (my examples).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Activity 4 and the best habit</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Check with a dictionary if there are other verbs that are used in the same form in all tenses” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1752s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:12</a>); a few to check (mine): <em>hit, hurt, let, set</em>. But you cannot check every verb. “The best thing to do, therefore, is to pay some attention to the form of words in use when reading or listening to anything.” “In every language only a few words have exceptional forms and they are used frequently”, so you learn them fast once you notice them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">How to learn from use</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Spend time with good English every day, “even if it is 10 minutes”: good broadcasts and telecasts, good documentaries or feature films, good books and journals. While doing it, notice “not just meaning but also the language”. With about an hour a day of “good, interesting, authentic stuff”, you can become a very good user of English within the semester (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>). As the lecturer often says, “excellence is the product of constant pursuit” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z8L7vIbAVK0&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"common-errors-2-subject-verb-agreement-and-the-head\">Common errors 2: subject–verb agreement and the head</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 10.2 looks at sentence types that are often heard “in the speech of educated speakers” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>). The sessions are “not exhaustive but indicative”: they show you an area to study, and you do more of it yourself (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</p>\n<p>“English grammar doesn’t have too many rules.” It has the rule of <strong>word order</strong> and the rule of <strong>subject–verb agreement</strong> in number (and agreement in tense); “other things are matters of usage” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). Agreement is “a very holy rule”: like entering a temple with your shoes on, breaking it stops people from taking you seriously as a user of English (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>). Agreement itself was taught in <a href=\"#/english1/w05/subject-verb-agreement\">Week 5</a>; this lecture adds <strong>how to find the subject</strong> in a long phrase.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Seven sentences with one common error (the lecture’s slides at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:50</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=259s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:19</a>; answers at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=434s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:14</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>✗ As on the slide</th>\n<th>✓ The answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a. Some boys of Rajendra Hostel <strong>is</strong> making all the noise.</td>\n<td>Some boys of Rajendra Hostel <strong>are</strong> making all the noise.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>b. Only one of the six scheduled trains <strong>are</strong> running late this morning.</td>\n<td>Only one of the six scheduled trains <strong>is</strong> running late this morning.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>c. Only one of the requested items <strong>were</strong> delivered late.</td>\n<td>Only one of the requested items <strong>was</strong> delivered late.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>d. The entire team with all its star players <strong>have</strong> been found guilty of doping.</td>\n<td>The entire team with all its star players <strong>has</strong> been found guilty of doping.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>e. We ordered six copies of this book, but <strong>it is</strong> yet to be delivered.</td>\n<td>We ordered six copies of this book, but <strong>they are</strong> yet to be delivered.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>f. Puurna’s collection of old folk songs <strong>are</strong> likely to be published soon.</td>\n<td>Puurna’s collection of old folk songs <strong>is</strong> likely to be published soon.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>g. All excepting one sailor <strong>was</strong> stranded on the island.</td>\n<td>(no answer on the slide; see below)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“All sentences above are ungrammatical because of non-agreement of the verbs in them with their subjects” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=259s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:19</a>). In (e) the error is in a <strong>pronoun</strong>: what is yet to come is the six <strong>copies</strong>, so <em>they are</em>, not <em>it is</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</p>\n<p>For (g), by the lecture’s rule: the head is <em>all</em> (all the sailors), which is plural, so ✓ <em>All excepting one sailor <strong>were</strong> stranded on the island.</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">What went wrong</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“In all of the sentences above, verb does not agree with the number of the subject. Here verb is influenced by the noun nearby, and is wrongly assigned the same number as that of the noun near it” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=677s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:17</a>). <strong>Number</strong> means singular (one) or plural (more than one).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Noun phrase, head and modifier</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>noun phrase</strong> is a group of words built around one noun: <em>some boys of Rajendra Hostel</em>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>head</strong> is that main noun: <em>boys</em>. It decides the number of the phrase.</li>\n<li>A <strong>modifier</strong> is a word or phrase that adds information about the head: <em>some</em>, <em>of Rajendra Hostel</em>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>determiner</strong> is a word before the noun that shows which one or how many: <em>a, the, some</em>. In <em>Puurna’s collection</em>, <em>Puurna’s</em> works “like a determiner” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The rule (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=717s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:57</a>): “Regardless of how many modifiers there are, it is always the head to which the verb agrees in number. So find the head.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Sentences (a) and (b), step by step (the lecture’s explanation at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> <em>Some boys of Rajendra Hostel ___ making all the noise.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>The word just before the verb is <em>Hostel</em>, which is singular. The speaker “has been misled by the word immediately before the verb.”</li>\n<li>Find the noun phrase: <em>some boys of Rajendra Hostel</em>.</li>\n<li>Find its head: <em>some</em> is an adjective, <em>of Rajendra Hostel</em> is a phrase that modifies <em>boys</em>, so the head is <strong>boys</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Boys</em> is plural: two boys, 20 boys, 200 boys.</li>\n<li>So the verb is plural: <strong>are</strong> making.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> <em>Only one of the six scheduled trains ___ running late this morning.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>The word just before the verb is <em>trains</em> (plural), but the verb is not decided by it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</li>\n<li>The head is <strong>one</strong>: “one of the trains” is a single train.</li>\n<li><em>One</em> is singular, so the verb is singular: <strong>is</strong> running.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) <em>are</em>; (b) <em>is</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">The nearest-noun trap</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Many people are “over-influenced by the noun immediately before” the verb (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>). In (c) <em>items</em> is near the verb, but only <strong>one</strong> item was late; in (d) <em>players</em> is near, but the head is <strong>team</strong>, which is singular; in (f) <em>songs</em> is near, but the head is <strong>collection</strong>, which is singular (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"how-to-find-the-head\">How to find the head</h3>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Are there any rules?” First, <strong>intuition</strong>: nobody wears a name plate in real life, yet we work out who is who. In the army, stars on the uniform show the rank; in real life there is no uniform, and we use intelligence and intuition (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>). Second, some rules of phrase structure: language is not mathematics, but “eighty percent of the phenomena in language is rule governed”, some say 85 to 90 percent (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Rule 1: noun + noun</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“In a noun + noun combination, ordinarily the <strong>second</strong> noun is the head” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=851s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:11</a>): <em>History <strong>Teacher</strong>, State <strong>Department</strong>, Home <strong>Minister</strong>, House <strong>Wife</strong>, Cinema <strong>Hall</strong>, etc.</em> The lecture adds <em>police<strong>man</strong></em>, <em>booking <strong>counter</strong></em>, <em>district <strong>magistrate</strong></em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>). The first noun only says what kind: a history teacher is a <strong>teacher</strong>.</p>\n<p>(The lecturer adds that in older English <em>wife</em> simply meant a woman (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>).)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Rule 2: noun + preposition + noun</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“But in a noun phrase created with a preposition, it is a noun <strong>before</strong> the preposition that is usually the head” (the same slide): <em><strong>Inspector</strong> of Police, <strong>Captain</strong> of Guards, <strong>House</strong> of Cards, <strong>Head</strong> of Department, <strong>Secretary</strong> to the Director, etc.</em> Also <em>the <strong>roses</strong> from Bangalore</em>, <em>the <strong>mango jelly</strong> from Vijayawada</em>, <em>the <strong>Chief Minister</strong> of Andhra Pradesh</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</p>\n<p>The lecture’s pair (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>The <strong>son</strong> of Dasaratha <strong>was</strong> exiled.</em> (one son)</li>\n<li><em>The <strong>sons</strong> of Pandu <strong>were</strong> exiled.</em> (many sons)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The noun after <em>of</em> (<em>Dasaratha</em>, <em>Pandu</em>) does not change the verb.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Find the head (the lecture’s Activity 2, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=717s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:57</a>; answers at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=1168s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:28</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Head</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a. The Geography Master was absent.</td>\n<td><strong>Master</strong></td>\n<td>noun + noun: the second noun</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>b. The boys of the First A had leisure between three and three-forty-five on Wednesday.</td>\n<td><strong>boys</strong></td>\n<td>the noun before <em>of</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>c. The noise that they make, sitting on their benches and swinging their legs, gets on his nerves.</td>\n<td><strong>noise</strong></td>\n<td>a long clause follows <em>noise</em>, but “not benches, not legs, but noise”, so <em>gets</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>d. No one, except Rajam and Mani, was there.</td>\n<td><strong>one</strong> (no one)</td>\n<td><em>Rajam and Mani</em> “come as second thoughts”; <em>no one</em> is the subject, so <em>was</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>e. Trucks full of water are now brought to cities from the villages.</td>\n<td><strong>Trucks</strong></td>\n<td>the noun before <em>full of</em>; plural, so <em>are</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>In (b) the verb <em>had</em> is the same for singular and plural, so only the head tells you the number (my remark).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Cover the extra words</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>To test a sentence, cover the modifiers and read what is left: <em>The noise [that they make, sitting on their benches and swinging their legs] <strong>gets</strong> on his nerves.</em> → <em>The noise gets on his nerves.</em> The lecturer suggests a similar practice: take noun phrases from any book, “remove in-between words”, and ask yourself which word is the head (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"agreement-in-a-story\">Agreement in a story</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Fill in the present tense (the lecture’s Activity 3 at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=1295s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:35</a>; answers at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=1442s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:02</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The task: “Fill in the blanks in the following sentences using the given verb in the present tense and in appropriate number.” The passage is about Swaminathan, his granny and his friends Rajam and Mani (characters of R. K. Narayan’s novel <em>Swami and Friends</em>; the slide does not name the book). The answers are in bold:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Every night after the meal, with his head on his granny’s lap, nestling close to her things Swaminathan <strong>feels</strong> (feel) very snug and safe in the faint atmosphere of cardamom and cloves. ‘Oh, granny!’ he <strong>cries</strong> (cry) ecstatically, ‘you don’t know what a great fellow Rajam is.’ He <strong>tells</strong> (tell) her the story of the first enmity between Rajam and Mani and the subsequent friendship. ‘<strong>Does</strong> (do) Granny <strong>know</strong> (know), Rajam has a real police dress,’ <strong>says</strong> (say) Swaminathan. ‘Is it? What <strong>does</strong> (do) he want a police dress for?’ asked granny. ‘His father is the Police Superintendent. He is the master of every policeman here.’ Granny is impressed. She says that it must be a tremendous office indeed.</p>\n<p>She then <strong>recounts</strong> (recount) the days when her husband, Swaminathan’s grandfather, was a powerful Sub-Magistrate, in which office he made the police force tremble before him, and the fiercest dacoits of the place fled. Swaminathan <strong>waits</strong> (wait) impatiently for her to finish the story. But she <strong>goes</strong> (go) on, rambled, confused, mixed up various incidents that took place at different times. ‘That will do, granny,’ he said ungraciously. ‘Let me tell you something about Rajam. <strong>Do</strong> (do) you know how many marks he <strong>gets</strong> (get) in Arithmetic?’ ‘He <strong>gets</strong> (get) all the marks, <strong>does</strong> (do) he, child?’ <strong>asks</strong> (ask) granny.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Why each answer:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Swaminathan, he, she, Granny</em> are singular (third person), so the present verb takes <em>-s</em>: <em>feels, cries, tells, says, recounts, waits, goes, gets, asks</em>.</li>\n<li>Spelling: <em>cry</em> → <em>cries</em> (<em>y</em> after a consonant becomes <em>ies</em>); <em>go</em> → <em>goes</em>.</li>\n<li>In a question with <em>do</em>, the <em>-s</em> goes on <em>do</em>: <em><strong>Does</strong> Granny <strong>know</strong></em>. “Because the singular has already been used”, the main verb takes its base (“neutral”) form: ✗ <em>Does Granny knows</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</li>\n<li>With <em>you</em>: <em><strong>Do</strong> you know</em>; in the tag <em>…, <strong>does</strong> he?</em> the subject is <em>he</em>, so <em>does</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Past events stay past</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>On the answer slide, the grandfather’s days stay in the past: <em>was a powerful Sub-Magistrate</em>, <em>made the police force tremble</em>, <em>fled</em>. They are over; only the story’s “now” moves to the present. Other verbs that were not blanks, such as <em>rambled</em> and <em>said</em>, are also left as they are.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Practise with any passage</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Take another passage, do them again … until such a time as you have got it” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>). “Nothing teaches you anything better than doing it yourself” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LNczGDRKX6Y&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>): rewrite the sentences, check your work with a book of usage or a dictionary, and if you are right, “pat yourself on the back”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"what-makes-writing-good\">What makes writing good</h2>\n<h3 id=\"a-short-history-of-writing\">A short history of writing</h3>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 10.3 opens the writing part of the course with a slide of four pictures in a row: a hard surface with scratched signs, a quill and an inkpot, a fountain pen, a laptop (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=14s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:14</a>). “The tools for writing have changed”, but writing is young: “many people say that until about 3000 years ago, until about a thousand years before Christ, mankind did not write” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s story of writing:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The first signs.</strong> About 1000 BC, “it is said”, some <strong>Phoenician sailors</strong> found that they could scratch signs on a stone slab, a slate or any hard surface with a pointed stone, and give each sign a value: a straight horizontal line meant one thing, a vertical line another; a circle one thing, a semicircle open to the right another (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>A merchant’s need.</strong> With these signs they remembered how much they had given to whom, how much they had to collect, what was in the store, what was sold, what they had invested and what profit to expect. “Like many other pieces of technology, this was also invented by the felt needs of merchants” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>New surfaces and tools.</strong> Hard surfaces gave place to leaves, barks, cloth and metal plates; the stone gave place to other pointed tools (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Paper.</strong> About 2000 years ago, “it is said”, the Chinese made paper out of a grass that grew in the river Nile, called <em>papyrus</em>, and that gave us the word <em>paper</em>. The lecturer adds that “there are more guesses than hard evidence for this kind of thing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Pens.</strong> Shakespeare wrote with a <strong>holder pen</strong> and had to dip it in the inkpot after every line. Writers such as Eliot and Bernard Shaw filled a <strong>fountain pen</strong> once and wrote for hours (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Today.</strong> We write on laptops, without paper, ink or pen, and we can change and edit easily (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">What historians say</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture tells the story the way “many people say”. Most historians date the first writing to about 5,000 years ago, in Mesopotamia and Egypt; the Phoenicians, about 3,000 years ago, made one of the first <strong>alphabets</strong>. Papyrus was a reed of the river Nile used by the Egyptians; paper as we know it was invented in China about 2,000 years ago from plant fibres, and its English name comes from <em>papyrus</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The basic technology of writing</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The tools change, but the basic technology does not (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>). You still need:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>a <strong>surface</strong>;</li>\n<li><strong>symbols</strong>;</li>\n<li><strong>tools</strong> that put the symbols on the surface;</li>\n<li>and the “<strong>software</strong>”: the rules that say in what order the symbols come and what they mean together.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"why-writing-matters\">Why writing matters</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Writing lasts long and goes far</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The first of the lecture’s basic points (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=387s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:27</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“<strong>Writing lasts long and goes far.</strong>”</li>\n<li>“A written text is easy to read, re-read, revise, and re-write.” A recorded speech can be rewound, “but not as easily” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</li>\n<li>“Writing has, therefore, been the medium of communication in business, sciences and technology, art and literature for centuries.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That is why writing is “perhaps the most essential skill”. Nobody pities a man who cannot cook or drive, but a person who cannot write is called <strong>illiterate</strong>, and UNESCO and governments run classes for them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>). All the world’s knowledge is kept in writing, in books, archives and libraries, and public and private <strong>documents</strong> are written so that later “you can say what was said, what was agreed upon” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-basics-of-good-writing\">The basics of good writing</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Point 2: content comes first</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Most important thing for writing well is its <strong>content</strong>” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=559s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:19</a>). Not the shape and size of the letters, not the rules of grammar, not a wide vocabulary (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“Do you have something to say? Lots of people are good at languages, but they have nothing to say.”</li>\n<li>“On the other hand, there are those who may not be good at languages, but have things to say. They are read and heard by all.”</li>\n<li>“Great poets, saints and scientists in all languages are of this kind.”</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Kabir Das and Shakespeare (the lecture’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=667s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:07</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Kabir Das</strong> (1398–1518): “It is said that Kabir Das, the 15th century Indian poet, was illiterate.” He was a weaver (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>William Shakespeare</strong> (1564–1616): “Shakespeare, the British poet, had hardly been to a primary school.” (That is the slide’s claim; Lecture 11.8 says he went to grammar school.) He wrote in English, not in Latin or French, the languages of the elite of his time.</li>\n<li>Both had things to say, so they became great writers, read even today. The lecture also names Kalidas, the Holy Quran, the Gita, the Mahabharata, the Ramayana, the Upanishads, and Homer’s <em>Iliad</em> and <em>Odyssey</em>: great content, read for generations (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Even an ordinary mail needs content (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>To write a mail to a librarian, “please send me the autobiography of Jean-Paul Sartre”, you must know three things:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>that you want an <strong>autobiography</strong>;</li>\n<li>the <strong>name of the writer</strong>, Jean-Paul Sartre;</li>\n<li>that the book <strong>is in the library</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Gather your content</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Gather as much information about your topic as you can before you begin writing. A good piece of writing is not mechanical. When you research and write, you do a better job” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=711s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:51</a>). Nobody knows everything at the start: “as we write … we research, we ask, we gather”. “An <strong>open mind</strong> is a great tool for a writer” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Point 3: small words and simple sentences</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“All great works, that have been read for centuries, in any discipline, [are] written in the simplest possible language” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=803s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:23</a>). Some people believe that long sentences and <strong>bombastic</strong> words (big-sounding words) make better writing; the lecture says the opposite (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Small words and simple sentences are understood by all.</li>\n<li>They also leave you less room for mistakes: “it is hardly likely that you make a mistake when you write simple sentences” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Prefer <strong>monosyllabic</strong> (one-syllable) and <strong>bisyllabic</strong> (two-syllable) words: <em>see</em>, not <em>visualise</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">One question, two ways (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>Can you visualise the contours of the design of this project?</em></li>\n<li><em>Can you see the outlines of this house?</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p>“You’re still saying the same thing”, but in small words: <em>see</em> for <em>visualise</em>, <em>outlines</em> for <em>contours</em> and <em>designs</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Text a and text b (the lecture’s slides at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=925s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:25</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=947s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:47</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>a.</strong> The project site was in great disarray and all manners of tools were strewn all over. The project director came for an unannounced visit on a particularly rainy day, took photographs and issued a memo to the project manager ordering action on the illumination front without delay, and the order was promptly complied with.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>b.</strong> The earth was without form, and void; and darkness was upon the face of the deep. And the Spirit of God moved upon the face of the waters. And God said, let there be light: and there was light.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li>“Both a. and b. describe the same situation”: darkness, an order for light, and light. Text a puts it in office words: <em>illumination front</em>, <em>promptly complied with</em>.</li>\n<li>Text b is from the <strong>King James Bible</strong> (“King’s Bible”), which the slide says was “written in English at the instance of King James I of England in 1612” and “is read by masses even today” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>). Its makers, a committee of about 40 people, the lecturer says, chose to use “hardly any jargon, no cliché”, so that everyone, scholars and lay people, adults and children, could understand.</li>\n<li>Count them (my count): a has <strong>54 words in 2 sentences</strong> (27 words a sentence); b has <strong>39 words in 3 sentences</strong> (13 words a sentence), and only 6 of its 39 words have more than one syllable (<em>without, darkness, upon, Spirit, upon, waters</em>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> “b. is so much easier to understand. With some regular practice, you can also learn to write in this manner” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">The date</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Most sources date the King James Bible to <strong>1611</strong>; the slide says 1612.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Point 4: writing is a skill</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“<strong>Writing is a skill</strong>, just as walking, eating, shaving, cycling, driving, swimming, typing or any other skill is. Regular practice is important. PLEASE, REMEMBER ONLY YOU CAN TEACH YOURSELF HOW TO WRITE” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=1026s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:06</a>).</p>\n<p>Watching many videos of people on bicycles will not teach you to ride one. Someone may push you or run beside you for a while, “but eventually you have to … start doing it on your own” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>). So write every day, even “only 15 minutes”, and <strong>revise</strong>: “what you wrote yesterday should be revised today” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Point 5: a good script comes after several drafts</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>draft</strong> is one version of a piece of writing before the final one. “Some writers, such as <strong>Ernest Hemingway</strong> (1899–1961), an American novelist and short story writer, or <strong>R K Narayan</strong> (1906–2001), an Indian English novelist and short story writer, are said to have made up to a dozen drafts of books they wrote” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=1201s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:01</a>). Hemingway, a Nobel Prize winner, began as a newspaper reporter in the First World War; Narayan became popular worldwide for the simplicity of his language and his sensitivity (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The cobbler’s glue</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer tells of a Harvard professor of economics who was President Kennedy’s ambassador to New Delhi when Nehru was Prime Minister, and his advice to writers: sit on a chair that has <strong>cobbler’s glue</strong> on it (the strong glue a shoemaker uses), so that you cannot get up until you have written your target of four or five pages (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>). As you sit and write, your writing improves.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Point 6: formats of writing</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Literary works, like poems, plays, stories and novels: free style. Essays and short paragraphs: have their own formats” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=1259s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:59</a>). A <strong>format</strong> is a fixed shape that a kind of writing follows. The slide’s samples: business letter, report, essay, short paragraph, mail, memo, PPT. The lecturer also names telegrams, WhatsApp messages, graphs and charts, and says the course will look at formats later (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Point 7: the same basics for everything</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“This course will teach how to write correct and easy to write and understand language. No matter what you write, you will need to write in these manners” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>): content is important, simple sentences help, revise and rewrite, practise daily, use simple words (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s activity</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Using your own ideas, arguments and evidence, write an essay “in about 250 words, i.e. in about one and a half pages, on ‘Future of Writing’”, in your own words, based on the four photographs at the start (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=1402s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:22</a>; in speech the lecturer says “the story of writing”). For more, the slide gives two Wikipedia pages: <em>History of writing</em> and <em>List of languages by first written accounts</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=1436s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:56</a>). Keep to 250 words: “you don’t have unlimited space, just as in speaking you don’t have unlimited time” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJN3-yzAhqU&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"bio-notes\">Bio-notes</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>We write because we have something to say; very few people write “only to play with words” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). The easiest content to start with is <strong>yourself</strong>: “nobody knows more about you than you yourself” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). You need to write about yourself when you apply for a job, are invited to give a talk, go for an interview, or visit another country (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Bio-note and CV</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Curriculum vitae (CV)</th>\n<th>Bio-note</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>What</td>\n<td>a <strong>detailed</strong> account of all you have done: work, learning, related things</td>\n<td>a <strong>short</strong> note about you</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Length</td>\n<td>as long as needed</td>\n<td>“sometimes … only 50 words, very often a hundred words” (also 150 or 200)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Content</td>\n<td>dates, places, lists</td>\n<td>“without dates, without particulars of time and place”, what <strong>sort of person</strong> you are</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(From the lecture at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>.) The challenge: “what can you say in a hundred words?”</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">A good bio-note</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A good bio-note “very briefly, without dates, without particulars of time and place, tells the reader what sort of a person the writer is” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). Ask: what makes me <strong>different</strong> from others, what sets me apart? Those things may be <strong>failures</strong> or <strong>successes</strong>; what matters is that you write about them <strong>objectively</strong> (as facts, without praising yourself). Because the space is small, it needs “great precision” and “good control on language” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"mission-and-vision-statements\">Mission and vision statements</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Mission, vision and value statements</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>mission statement</strong> says what a person or an organisation sets out to do.</li>\n<li>A <strong>vision statement</strong> says what they want to become in the future.</li>\n<li>A <strong>value statement</strong> says the principles they follow.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Many people now begin a bio-note or a CV with a mission or vision statement. “It is in fashion these days” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=376s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:16</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">You need not follow the fashion</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In the lecturer’s “very personal opinion”, a young person, “still forming, still learning”, need not write a mission or vision statement (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>). Your aim now may simply be a good university, a good job, a good family, a good house: “these things are not sinful”. Not following the fashion “itself will make you different” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>). The difference between a mission and a vision statement is “razor thin”; “let organisations do that” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</p>\n<p>If your aims <strong>are</strong> clear, say them, but in simple, <strong>concrete</strong> words, not in clichés (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Cliché</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A word or phrase “which has often been repeated by everyone”, so that it “has lost its meaning” and “doesn’t have a concrete meaning” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">“A great career in management” (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>I want a great career in management.</em> What is <em>great</em>? What is <em>management</em>? “Everyone wants a great career in management.”</li>\n<li>Gandhiji’s career was great: he managed the freedom struggle of a whole country, and nobody before him had done anything like it. Just being elected chief minister of a state is not “great” in that sense (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Concrete instead: <em>In the next five years I want to get a job which will give me 200,000 rupees a month</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>). Or: <em>In the next five years I want to become a middle-level manager</em>, or <em>the head of the company</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>). Or: most people have not written 30 books on a subject; <em>I want to write 31 books on this subject</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Ambition is not wrong: “if you do not want to steal from somebody else”, even wanting to be a multi-billionaire is a noble ambition (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The Jailee Country statements (the lecture’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=704s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:44</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Mission Statement.</strong> Jailee Country government is dedicated to delivering effective, efficient, respectful public service with integrity and accountability using both proven and innovative methods.</p>\n<p><strong>Vision Statement.</strong> To make Jailee Country the place of choice to live, work, grow and play.</p>\n<p><strong>Value Statements.</strong> We are responsible, respectful, accountable, effective, efficient public servants. We promote integrity and openness in everything we do.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>What the lecture finds:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The words</th>\n<th>The problem</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>dedicated to delivering … public service</em></td>\n<td>no government can say it does <strong>not</strong> want to serve the public (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>effective, efficient, respectful</em></td>\n<td><strong>redundant</strong>: “you cannot be effective unless you are efficient”, and if you are both, people will respect you anyway (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>with integrity and accountability</em></td>\n<td>if you are accountable, integrity is already there (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>using both proven and innovative methods</em></td>\n<td>why not <em>using old and new methods</em>? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>the place of choice to live, work, grow and play</em></td>\n<td><strong>padding</strong>: <em>live</em> already includes work, play, growth (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Redundancy, padding and verbosity</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Redundancy</strong>: words that repeat what is already said, “packing it with unnecessary words” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>). It may suit political speeches; “in serious business or academic writing, redundancy is not a virtue” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Padding</strong>: unnecessary words added only to fill space; “they become distractors rather than communicators” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Verbose</strong>: using lots of words, many of them redundant or inaccurate, that “don’t convey anything” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Accuracy</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Use “just the word required, accurate word, no other word”: “only a particular button will fit a particular hole”; only a certain size of nut fits a certain bolt; “you cannot put a round peg in a square hole” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"choosing-what-to-say\">Choosing what to say</h3>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Budget your words</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Like we budget our money”: with 100 rupees for a week, you buy water and bread first, and perhaps no pizza or Coke. With 150 words for 16 years of life, ask what is <strong>more</strong> important (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s question (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1181s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:41</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“In about 150 words, write a note on important events in your life. You must be factual. Tell the reader how you are different.”</p>\n<p>Hints: <em>Name – basic address – early education – any notable events – college – favourite subjects – achievements – plans for the future.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Name</strong>: always.</li>\n<li><strong>Address</strong>: one or two words, such as your country or state (<em>I am from Nepal</em>, <em>I am from Sikkim</em>); the full address goes elsewhere (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Early childhood</strong>: about 25 words, if it was different (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>College</strong>: the subject you enjoyed most; a cup, a medal, a prize; why you did well in one subject and not in another (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Future</strong>: a lot of money, cars and big holidays, or “a simple austere life”; “they are equally good” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Childhoods worth describing (the lecture’s stories at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>A colleague’s father cleaned a public washroom. The boy worked hard and became an orthopaedic surgeon. What in that home, with uneducated parents, motivated the child? That is worth describing.</li>\n<li>A Vietnamese English-teacher colleague lost her father; her mother ran a small confectionery shop (biscuits, candies). From the age of eight, after school, the girl ran to the shop and kept its accounts. She learned accounting, English and mathematics (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</li>\n<li>A failure is worth mentioning too, if you reached somewhere in spite of it. A famous inventor said that each of his inventions came after nine failures (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"two-drafts-of-one-bio-note\">Two drafts of one bio-note</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Baagmati’s bio-note (the lecture’s slides at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1502s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:02</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1538s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:38</a> and the comparison at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1675s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:55</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Both drafts tell the same life: a young woman from a Himalayan village (from Nepal, the lecturer says) who comes to Mumbai and builds a new life (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>). The slides compare them sentence by sentence; a dash means the final draft drops the sentence.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>First draft</th>\n<th>Final draft</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>My name is Baagmati. It mean one with speech.</td>\n<td>My name is Baagmati.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I was born at a village in the hills of the Himalayas, but I do not know in which year.</td>\n<td>I was born at a village in the Himalayas.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>But I was never sent to school. Girls did not go to school in my village. “That is a waste of time”, my mother said. Girls were however taught painting, singing, cooking and taking care of household. I have done these things quite well. I was married young.</td>\n<td>I was never sent to school, but I was taught arts and household work.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>After three years of marriage, I went to Mumbai along with my husband.</td>\n<td>After three years of marriage, I went to Mumbai with my husband by rail.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>It was a long train journey, for three days and nights, we saw many towns and villages</td>\n<td>It was a long and interesting journey.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>In Mumbai, my husband worked as a security guard at the gate of a big building, I worked in the flat of a businessman.</td>\n<td>In Mumbai, I worked in the home of a businessman.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>They were very kind to us.</td>\n<td>–</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>They allowed us to live in their servants’ quarters.</td>\n<td>He gave us a place to live</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I used to clean the flat and help my mistress in washing clothes, plates and in cooking.</td>\n<td>I used to help his wife at home.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>But after some years my husband died suddenly one day. No one said what disease he had.</td>\n<td>But my husband soon died of some strange disease.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The businessman did not throw me out. They allowed me to live and work as usual.</td>\n<td>Then I was confused and doubtful of my future, but I lived and worked as usual.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I had also learnt from their children how to read and write Hindi and English.</td>\n<td>From the businessman’s children, I had learnt reading, writing and some computer skills.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>So after the death of my husband I decided to write school final examination, and passed in the first attempt. Then I also joined college and passed plus two examination. With help from the same family, I have also acquired some computer skills.</td>\n<td>So, after some studies, I wrote and passed school final examination, and joined junior college for plus two class.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Now I want to become a graduate and start a computer Centre for girls at my village.</td>\n<td>I wish to learn more of computers, and after graduation, I wish to go back to my village and start a computer school for girls.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I am sure God will help me.</td>\n<td>–</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The numbers (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1583s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:23</a>; I recounted both drafts and got the same):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>First draft</th>\n<th>Final draft</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Words</td>\n<td>278</td>\n<td>155</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sentences</td>\n<td>25</td>\n<td>13</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ol>\n<li>Words cut: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">278</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">155</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">123</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li>Part cut: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">278</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">123</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.44</span></span></span></span>,</span></span> that is, about 44 words in every 100.</li>\n<li>The final draft is close to the limit of “about 150 words”, and it still tells the whole story (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the same life in 155 words instead of 278: the final draft “has done away with lots of redundant words, redundant phrases, and most words are used very accurately” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two rules for every draft</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide “Redundancies and inaccuracies” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1658s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:38</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Remove redundant words and phrases.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Use words accurately.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">What the lecturer says about the first rows (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>It mean one with speech</em> is “an oversight” (a small slip): it should be <em>It means</em>. There is no other error of this kind in the text.</li>\n<li><em>My name is Baagmati</em> (4 words) could be <em>I am Baagmati</em> (3 words): one word saved.</li>\n<li>Why give the <strong>meaning</strong> of the name? “The primary function of a name is to identify a person or place or thing.” A place called Heaven Lake is still a lake; the institute could have another name and still be the same institute. The meaning is redundant (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>But I was never sent to school. Girls did not go to school in my village …</em> can be just <em>I was never sent to school</em>, or <em>I never went to school</em>. Then one line on what her early education gave her: some arts and skill in household work (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>“Otherwise, in the flood of redundant words, you might lose your reader” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Write, rewrite</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“A good piece of writing is not just a mechanical stuff. You write, you rewrite, you rewrite; you look at spelling, you look at words, you look at sentences, you add something, delete something else” until it is all right (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"a-senior-academic-s-bio-note\">A senior academic’s bio-note</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">“Why I will be the best Vice-Chancellor?” (the lecture’s slides at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1871s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:11</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=2042s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:02</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A senior academic wrote this as part of her application to be the vice-chancellor (head) of a university (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>). Her career is good; the note is not. Some of its sentences:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I have been privileged to study under great scholars who enabled me to aspire for the highest academic post. I got first class first in my master degree, I have Ph.D and D.Litt degrees in my subject. … Being chairman of Admission committees, I have attracted and admitted more students in various courses of the university.. Hence our campus has 18000 students. … I continue to hold many administrative positions. I am chairman of many committees …</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>What the lecturer finds:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The words</th>\n<th>The problem and the fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>great scholars</em></td>\n<td>vague: name them. We know Alexander studied under Aristotle; if you studied under a Nobel Prize winner, say so (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>the highest academic post</em></td>\n<td>vague: which post? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>I got first class first in my master degree</em></td>\n<td>simpler: <em>I topped my class</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Ph.D and D.Litt degrees</em></td>\n<td>already in the detailed CV (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>attracted and admitted</em></td>\n<td>redundant: you could not admit them without attracting them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=2040s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>more students in various courses</em></td>\n<td>obvious for a chairman of admissions (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=2070s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>I continue to hold many administrative positions</em></td>\n<td>simpler: <em>I still hold …</em>, or <em>I also have some administrative positions</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=2100s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 35:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the list of posts (Gymkhana, Chief Vigilance Officer, Dean)</td>\n<td>“could go in a box somewhere else” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=2130s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 35:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The lecture’s activity: “Make a list of redundancies and inaccuracies in this draft. Re-write a draft without them” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=2047s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:07</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Tell stories, not lists</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Use the bio-note for what cannot go in a table or a bullet list: “the unusual decisions that you took, the unusual pressures that you have withstood, the outstanding teachers under whom you studied, the way you helped an ordinary student become good or great”, how you managed a conflict in a committee and still reached a unanimous decision. “Some of these stories would have made a great bio note, not a random listing of facts with redundant words” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=2130s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 35:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Questions to ask while revising</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“What can go better here? What can wait? Is this the best way to write this? Is this the best sentence? Is this the best word? Does this sentence fit with the one that has gone before, and lead to the next?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5JkSz17l1Ao&amp;t=2220s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:00</a>). At first a 200-word note may take you one, two or even three days; that time trains you.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"describing-a-photograph\">Describing a photograph</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Language is mainly spoken, but we often <strong>write</strong> to describe something we have seen for people who were not there (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). Before cameras, travellers described strange lands in words: foreigners in India saw trees with hanging roots and animals whose nose touched the ground (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). A child from New Delhi visiting a village in Bihar saw creepers on a roof and cried, “Mama, see, there is vegetable on the roof!” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>). What is common to one person is new to another, so a description must show it clearly.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The task</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Look at the following photograph carefully, and then in no more than 150 words, describe all that you see” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=214s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:34</a>).</p>\n<p>The word limit: 150 words does not mean 10 words. “It could be 145 words, it could be 155, but no more and no less”: about 5 words either way (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"look-before-you-write\">Look before you write</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">What the lecturer sees (the photograph at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=255s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:15</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Some people would say “this is the ladies compartment” and stop. Take about a minute and look (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The place:</strong> a compartment of a railway train, perhaps in India, but it could be Bangladesh, Pakistan, Nepal or another South Asian country (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>The passengers:</strong> all ladies and small girls; perhaps one boy in front (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Their clothes:</strong> children in frocks; almost all adults in saris, in red, orange, pink, black, purple, green, white (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Seats:</strong> nearly all occupied; about half a dozen passengers standing (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Hand grips</strong> hang from a rail on the ceiling, but no standing passenger holds one (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Ceiling fans:</strong> so the train runs in a hot, humid, tropical country, not in Europe or North America (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>The coach:</strong> the design of the seats, the ventilation (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>What cannot be known:</strong> is the train coming towards us or going away? The photo gives no clue, so leave it out (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> look at everything: “passengers, fittings, furnishing, the design of the coach”. Describe what is <strong>in</strong> the picture.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"two-drafts\">Two drafts</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The first draft (the lecture’s slides at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=691s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:31</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<blockquote>\n<p>The photograph presents a view of a second class ladies’ compartment in a railway train in India. Most probably it is a local train commuting between the suburbs and the city, bringing the ladies to work and taking them back to their homes.</p>\n<p>The compartment appears to be a fairly large one, and crowded, it seems lots of ladies and children are packed in it like fish in cans. There also appear to be quite a few children, though it is not very clear what they are doing there.</p>\n<p>The compartment has also provided hand grips hanging from a tube attached to the roof of the compartment so that standing passengers can hold it and travel without falling. No lady passenger in this compartment is holding the grip just now. May be they do not mind falling!</p>\n<p>All the ladies there in the compartment are wearing very beautiful and colourful saris. They are red, pink, black, green, purple with matching blouses. They generally seem to be very happy and peaceful. Children are also very quiet. Though not all of them are seated. On the whole it is a very typically Indian situation.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The final draft, as far as the slides show it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=766s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:46</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<blockquote>\n<p>This is a photograph of a ladies compartment in a railway train. All seats here are occupied. Some passengers are also standing. Hand grips hanging from the tube attached to the ceiling show that the compartment takes both, sitting passengers and some standing passengers. There seem to be above 50 passengers in the compartment.</p>\n<p>Most passengers are adults, and seem to be going to or coming from their day’s work. The compartment also has a few girls. It is not clear why they are there.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The slide shows only this first part (85 words, 8 sentences, by my count); the rest of the final draft is not shown. Some of its sentences appear in the comparison tables below.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The numbers (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=811s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:31</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>First draft</th>\n<th>Final draft</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Words</td>\n<td>192</td>\n<td>146</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sentences</td>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>14</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ol>\n<li>The highest count allowed is about 155 words, so the first draft is over by <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">192</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">155</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">37</span></span></span></span></span> words, “nearly 40 words more” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</li>\n<li>As a part of the limit: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">155</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">37</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.24</span></span></span></span>,</span></span> about “one-fourth more than there ought to be”.</li>\n<li>Words per sentence (my count): first draft <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">13</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">192</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">14.8</span></span></span></span>;</span></span> final draft <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">14</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">146</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10.4</span></span></span></span>.</span></span></li>\n<li>“With fewer words, the final draft has more sentences”: it has many <strong>simple</strong> sentences, so it is easier to understand (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the final draft is 46 words shorter <span class=\"nw\">(<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">192</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">146</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">46</span></span></span></span>),</span> inside the limit, and made of shorter sentences.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"what-the-final-draft-removed\">What the final draft removed</h3>\n<p>“A lot of mistakes in writing occur not because we do not know the language, but because we write more than necessary”, and unnecessarily difficult words and sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>). The comparison slides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=884s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:44</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=1312s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:52</a>), with the lecturer’s reasons:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>presents a view</em> → removed. A photograph can only present a view; say <em>This is a photograph of …</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>bringing the ladies to work and taking them back to their homes</em> → removed. It is <strong>redundant</strong>: <em>commute</em> already means to go to work and come back (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>It seems lots of ladies and children are packed in it like fish in cans.</em> → removed. It is a <strong>cliché</strong> (people say “like sardines”), and it is not true: nobody is pushing anyone. <em>Lots</em> says nothing definite (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>No lady passenger in this compartment …</em> → <em>No passenger …</em>. <em>Lady</em> is redundant in a ladies compartment, and misleading: are men holding the grips? <em>Just now</em> is also redundant, because <em>is holding</em> already means now (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>May be they do not mind falling!</em> → removed. It is a bad joke, “an insult to the people you describe” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>All the ladies there in the compartment are wearing very beautiful and colourful saris.</em> → <em>All ladies/passengers are wearing colourful saris.</em> <em>There in the compartment</em> is “a very painful elaboration of the obvious”; <em>very beautiful</em> is subjective; <em>colourful</em> stays, because the photograph shows it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>They generally seem to be very happy and peaceful. Children are also very quiet. Though not all of them are seated. On the whole it is a very typically Indian situation.</em> → <em>They seem to be peaceful. Children are also quiet.</em> We cannot see whether they are <em>happy</em>; <em>peaceful</em> we can see, since nobody seems to talk or make noise. “A typically Indian situation” is vague (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Subjective and specific words</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>subjective</strong> word depends on the feeling of the person who uses it: <em>beautiful</em>, <em>difficult</em>, <em>very happy</em>. “What is beautiful for one may not be beautiful for another” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>). The lecture also calls such words <strong>attitudinal</strong>: they show your attitude, not a fact.</li>\n<li>A <strong>specific</strong> (verifiable) word names a fact anyone can check: “what is three kilometres for one is three kilometres also for another”. <em>That teacher is visually challenged, yet he teaches one of the most interesting classes in this school</em>: <em>visually challenged</em> is a fact. <em>Interesting</em> is fair only if, say, all students’ feedback says so (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Avoid adjectives if possible</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Too many adjectives and adverbs (<em>very</em>, <em>beautiful</em>) are “like roadblocks” in written language: they get in the way of communication. “A good principle is: avoid adjectives if possible”, and adverbs too. Keep a word like <em>colourful</em> only when the picture shows it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Lots</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Lots of women and children</em> tells the reader nothing definite; “it only indicates your inability to count”. Count them, or say <em>many</em>, <em>quite a few</em>, <em>above 50</em>. “When a photograph gives you a definite view, the writer … should give a concrete view to their reader” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"accurate-words\">Accurate words</h3>\n<p>The slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=1709s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:29</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Inaccurate</th>\n<th>Accurate</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a second class ladies’ compartment</td>\n<td>a ladies’ second class compartment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The compartment has also provided hand grips …</td>\n<td>The compartment has been provided with hand grips / The compartment has hand grips …</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Children are also very quiet. Though not all of them are seated.</td>\n<td>Though not all of them are seated, children are also very quiet.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Word order:</strong> the compartment is a second-class one <strong>for ladies</strong>, so <em>ladies’</em> comes first, like <em>a ladies’ first class compartment</em>, <em>a ladies’ air-conditioned coach</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Voice:</strong> a compartment cannot <em>provide</em> anything; the railways did. Since the focus is on the grips and not on the provider, use the <strong>passive voice</strong>: <em>has been provided with</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>). Or simply: <em>The compartment has hand grips.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Sentence:</strong> <em>Though not all of them are seated</em> cannot stand alone as a sentence, so join it to the main clause (my explanation).</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Active and passive voice</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In the <strong>active</strong> voice the doer is the subject: <em>The railways provided hand grips.</em> In the <strong>passive</strong> voice the thing acted on is the subject, and the doer may be left out: <em>The compartment has been provided with hand grips.</em> “In some places you will find active voice is accurate; in some other places … passive voice is more accurate” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Every word has a place</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Every word has a place and every place has a word”, like a square peg that fits only a square hole (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>). An inaccurate word may not be a grammar error, but “communication will be compromised”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s activity and checklist</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide gives a second photograph (a crowded celebration at night, with lights, decorations and people in colourful turbans, saris and suits) and asks you to describe it “in 150 words”: people, activities, decorations, lights, costumes, jewellery, the mood of the people, furniture and other props, colours, kinds of people (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=1891s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:31</a>). Then revise your first draft and ask: “Have I made any mistake of grammar, vocabulary, punctuation, spelling? Are the words inaccurate? Have I written very long sentences? Have I written words that are not necessary?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WMjTEHW6YsU&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"writing-for-public-purposes\">Writing for public purposes</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Describing a photograph for friends or family is <strong>private</strong> writing. When you write on a <strong>public</strong> issue, your text goes into the <strong>public domain</strong>: it has “no fixed reader”, so anyone who can read may read it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). We do this often, because public problems affect us all: limited water, electricity or cooking-gas supply, bad law and order, and pollution of many kinds: noise, gas and <strong>suspended particulate matter</strong> (tiny particles floating in the air), and too much light (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The four demands of public writing</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=229s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:49</a>: “On problems, public issues etc. Need for precision, brevity, facts, etc. Importance of being formal and factual.”</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Be …</th>\n<th>It means</th>\n<th>The lecture’s picture</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>precise</strong></td>\n<td>say all that is to be said, “no more, no less”</td>\n<td>like clothes: if your size is XL, you cannot take XXL or L (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>brief</strong></td>\n<td>“if something can be said in four words, then please do not say that in five words”</td>\n<td><em>God sees everything</em> → <em>God is omniscient</em> (knows everything); <em>God is there everywhere</em> → <em>God is omnipresent</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>factual</strong></td>\n<td>“write only facts, not attitudes, not hearsay”</td>\n<td>not <em>very beautiful saris</em>: the beauty is your attitude, the colour is a fact (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>formal</strong></td>\n<td>not casual: no slang, private idioms or jokes</td>\n<td>not <em>Hi Chairman</em> or <em>Hi guys</em>, even if the chairman is your friend; <em>I wish to draw your attention …</em>, <em>I beg to inform you …</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“All public documents are created in a style of writing which is formal, which is factual, which is precise and which is brief” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>). <strong>Hearsay</strong> means what you heard from others but cannot prove. <strong>Verbosity</strong> (using more words than needed) “takes away from the contents”, distracts the reader and may make you look funny (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The commuter train again (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Verbose: <em>It’s a commuter train which takes passengers every morning to their work and brings them back home every evening.</em></li>\n<li><em>Commuter train</em> already says that it runs between certain stations every day, taking people to work and home again.</li>\n<li>So the last two clauses are “utterly redundant”.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>It is a commuter train.</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"civic-problems\">Civic problems</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Civic problem</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Civic</em> means about a city or town and the people who live in it. “A civic problem is a man-made problem. Most problems in the world are man-made problems” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=682s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:22</a>). It is not a military problem (war), nor a natural one such as a famine or an earthquake, “though there is human hand even in them” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</p>\n<p>The slide’s examples: “<strong>crime, corruption, chaos, pollution, poverty, discrimination, exploitation, environmental problems, overconsumption</strong> etc.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecturer’s notes on them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Traffic jams</strong> on the roads.</li>\n<li><strong>Crime</strong>: partly because people are poorly educated, poor, unmotivated or unemployed. Rich, educated and employed people commit crimes too, but they are few.</li>\n<li><strong>Chaos</strong>: no rule of law; countries “reeling under civil war”, where children, women, the old and the sick suffer.</li>\n<li><strong>Pollution</strong>: too much gas and suspended particulate matter in the air; noise, light and water pollution; scarce drinking water.</li>\n<li><strong>Poverty and exploitation</strong>: of the poor by the rich, the disabled by the able, the weak by the mighty.</li>\n<li><strong>Environmental problems</strong>: falling oxygen levels and global warming from the constant use of carbon fuels.</li>\n<li><strong>Over-consumption</strong>: some people throw away food and clothes, while some schools and hospitals have no equipment because they have no money.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Writing about them “is not just a test only of language; it’s also a test of your ability to gather facts and to present them in a focused manner” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"planning-a-letter-on-waterlogging\">Planning a letter on waterlogging</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s question (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=945s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:45</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“In about 150 words, write a letter to the Chairman of your city corporation drawing their attention to the water logging on the road near your house.”</p>\n<p>Hint: <em>Problem of drainage – absence of municipal services – disturbance to traffic – hazards for pedestrians – health hazards to residents – unaesthetic look – cleaning and repair of drainage required – funds can be raised, etc. – importance of good roads.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Waterlogging</strong> is water that stands on a road or a field and does not flow away. <strong>Drainage</strong> is the system of drains that carries water away. A <strong>pedestrian</strong> is a person walking. <strong>Unaesthetic</strong> means not pleasant to look at.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecturer’s notes before writing (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Make your notes before you begin writing”:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The problem:</strong> when it rains, the water does not flow away; somewhere the drain is <strong>choked</strong> (blocked).</li>\n<li><strong>Why:</strong> keeping drains clear is a main duty of the city corporation, through regular cleaning and <strong>dredging</strong> (removing the mud from the bottom). Either this is not done, or people have <strong>encroached</strong> on the drains (built over land that is not theirs).</li>\n<li><strong>Harm to traffic:</strong> cars, cycles and people on foot cannot move easily or quickly.</li>\n<li><strong>Harm to pedestrians:</strong> they cannot see what is under the water: an open manhole, or a sharp object that can cut their feet.</li>\n<li><strong>Harm to health:</strong> mosquitoes and other pests that carry disease breed in standing, rotting water.</li>\n<li><strong>Harm to looks:</strong> however beautiful the city, waterlogged roads give it a bad name.</li>\n<li><strong>Remedies:</strong> regular, periodic maintenance and cleaning of the drains; funds can be raised (the government may help, and people may help themselves); cleaning and repair of the drains; good roads, free of potholes and water, so that a speeding car does not skid.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> a public document must also <strong>suggest</strong> what can be done (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Information gathering</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>For a public problem, you must first <strong>gather</strong> information; it is not all in front of you, as in a photograph (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>). The slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1425s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:45</a>: “statistical – anecdotal – local – global – general – particular – types of problem – quantities of each of them – number or unit wise, etc.”</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Statistical</strong>: numbers, such as how many roads in the city have potholes. You can survey one road and extend the result (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Anecdotal and local</strong>: what you saw on one road: how much delay waterlogging caused there, how much petrol the waiting vehicles burnt (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Global and general</strong>: wider data, if you have it, and then “particularise” it to your road (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Types of problem</strong>: on a road: traffic jams, stray animals, broken roads and potholes, no traffic signs (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Quantities</strong>: how many kilometres of road are broken, which places they connect (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>“It just requires a little bit of brainstorming and no more.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"a-shop-notice\">A shop notice</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">“Closed because it is closed” (the lecture’s photograph at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1259s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:59</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A friend photographed this notice outside a shop:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>THIS STOR IS NOT OPEN TODAY BECOS IT IS CLOSED.. BY: MENEJA</p>\n</blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Spelling errors:</strong> <em>stor</em> (store), <em>becos</em> (because), <em>meneja</em> (manager) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Convention errors:</strong> capital letters in the middle of a sentence, and two full stops.</li>\n<li><strong>Redundancy:</strong> “half of this text is redundant”. <em>Not open today</em> and <em>closed</em> say the same thing twice (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</li>\n<li>The writer could have said either <em>This store is not open today</em> or <em>This store is closed today by order of the manager</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> ✓ <em>This store is closed today by order of the manager.</em> “Redundancy is a great enemy of all writing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"two-drafts-of-the-letter\">Two drafts of the letter</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The first draft opens like this (the lecture’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1408s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:28</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<blockquote>\n<p>To: The Chairman, City Corporation of Swamitipurami, Chennai 600 002 — 26 Jan, 2019</p>\n<p>Sub : Water logging on road – reg.</p>\n<p>Sir,</p>\n<p>I wish to draw your attention to the unhygienic condition and traffic hazard prevailing in this area due to water logging on the main road here.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Only this opening is shown on the slide.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The final draft (the lecture’s slides at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1645s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:25</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<blockquote>\n<p>To: The Chairman,\nCity Corporation of Chennai,\nChennai 600 002 — 26 Jan, 2019</p>\n<p>Sub : Water logging on road – reg.</p>\n<p>Sir,</p>\n<p>Some action is urgently requested to remove water logging on the road here. It causes traffic hazard, insanitation, breeding of mosquitoes, and stench all around. Last week three vehicles got stuck, and had to be pulled out by crane. Two passengers have already been hurt. Some have complained of skin allergies. And many have complained and shouted at the drivers. Drivers splash water wetting the clothes of walkers. Cases of water borne disease are rising. A photograph is attached. Please, have the drains inspected and blockages removed, so that the accumulated water can flow away freely.</p>\n<p>The entire area must be sanitized. Damaged roads must be repaired and traffic marks redrawn. Residents may, please, be compensated for damages. Until some measures are taken, traffic may be diverted to another road. Water supply lines must also be checked for leakages. Laying new lines may be a welcome step.</p>\n<p>With best regards,\nSantosh Gupta</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>(<em>Sub</em> = subject; <em>reg.</em> = regarding. <em>Insanitation</em> = dirty, unhealthy conditions; <em>stench</em> = a very bad smell. The slide has the typo <em>skins allergies</em>, corrected here.) The attached photograph shows cars, buses and motorcycles moving slowly through a flooded road (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1655s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:35</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">How the final draft meets the hints (my analysis of the lecture’s letter)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Request first:</strong> the opening sentence asks for action, formally (<em>Some action is urgently requested …</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Traffic:</strong> <em>three vehicles got stuck, and had to be pulled out by crane</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pedestrians:</strong> <em>two passengers have already been hurt</em>; <em>drivers splash water wetting the clothes of walkers</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Health:</strong> <em>insanitation, breeding of mosquitoes, stench</em>; <em>skin allergies</em>; <em>cases of water borne disease are rising</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence:</strong> <em>a photograph is attached</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Remedies:</strong> drains inspected and blockages removed; the area sanitised; roads repaired and traffic marks redrawn; compensation; traffic diverted for now; water lines checked.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Every sentence is a fact or a request: formal, factual, precise, brief.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">What changed (the slides at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1669s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:49</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:10</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>First draft</th>\n<th>Final draft</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>291 words, little information</td>\n<td>148 words, lots of information</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the usual redundancies</td>\n<td>removed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>… and traffic hazard</em> (unclear)</td>\n<td>✗ as a vague phrase; facts instead</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Even a drizzle … rainfall</em></td>\n<td><em>Even a shower …</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Redundancy</strong>, as in the shop notice (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Unclear words:</strong> what does <em>traffic hazard</em> mean? Head-on collisions? People run over? Say what actually happens (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>No weather lecture:</strong> instead of drizzle, light rain and rainfall, just say that the slightest rainfall causes waterlogging on the road (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Sentence construction</strong> (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:10</a>):</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>First draft</th>\n<th>Final version</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Motor vehicles passing through this road have no idea of the depth or the condition of the road under water, and as they drive at their usual fast speed, they not only splash water all around, they also cause hazard to other vehicles and pedestrians nearby.</td>\n<td>Drivers have no idea of the road under water. They drive fast, splashing water. They are a threat to other drivers and vehicles.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“Motor vehicles don’t have an idea; drivers have an idea. So be accurate” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>). One long sentence becomes three short ones.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s activity</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Write a letter on any of the following topics to the editor of a local newspaper requesting them to highlight the issue raised by you” (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1787s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:47</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>light pollution in your residential locality;</li>\n<li>noise pollution in your area (a quiet area that has become noisy);</li>\n<li>supply of unclean water in your area;</li>\n<li>need of furniture for students;</li>\n<li>difficulties in managing mid-day meals.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Find a way to <strong>quantify</strong> (put a number or a measure on) the problem. For unclean water: “we cannot see the bottom of the cup if we fill it with water from this lake” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>). Take one topic a week, gather all the information you can, write “as many drafts as necessary”, then ask friends, family or a teacher to review it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>). “Excellence requires personal initiative” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AF8akyH8Duo&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Common errors 1</strong> (10.1): uncountable (mass) nouns take no <em>a/an</em> and no <em>-s/-es</em>; the dictionary marks them [U] (countable [C]): <em>equipment, meat, oil, rice, wheat, milk, information, fish, furniture, bread, petrol, sugar, tea, water</em>. Use them in the citation form. Amounts: <em>some information, a little knowledge, a news item, a loaf/slice/morsel of bread, a head of fish</em> (everyday English: <em>a piece of fish</em>), <em>a portion of meat, a gallon of petrol, a grain of rice, a litre of water</em>. Why not <em>breads</em>? Languages are arbitrary, like grammatical gender. <em>Appear, feel, have, look, seem, stand</em> are not used with <em>-ing</em> in the lecture’s rule (✓ <em>I feel all right</em>), except <em>having breakfast</em> (eat), <em>standing for election</em> (contest), <em>feeling the pulse</em> (touch); standard English also allows <em>I’m feeling fine</em> and <em>is standing at the post</em>. <em>Cast, cost, cut, put</em>: one form in all tenses (✓ <em>Votes have been cast</em>). Learn from use: notice the form of words when you read and listen.</p>\n<p><strong>Common errors 2</strong> (10.2): errors come from over-generalisation. The verb agrees in number with the <strong>head</strong> of the noun phrase, not the nearest noun: <em>Some boys of Rajendra Hostel are …</em>; <em>Only one of the six scheduled trains is …</em>; <em>The entire team with all its star players has …</em>; <em>Puurna’s collection of old folk songs is …</em>. Noun + noun: the second noun is the head (<em>history teacher</em>). Noun + preposition + noun: the noun before the preposition (<em>inspector of police</em>; <em>sons of Pandu were</em>). After <em>does</em>, the base form (<em>Does Granny know</em>).</p>\n<p><strong>Good writing</strong> (10.3): writing lasts long and goes far; easy to read, re-read, revise, rewrite. Content first (Kabir Das, Shakespeare); gather information. Small words and simple sentences (<em>see</em>, not <em>visualise</em>; text b of the King James Bible). Writing is a skill: practise daily; only you can teach yourself. A good script comes after several drafts (Hemingway, R. K. Narayan; the cobbler’s glue). Formats: free style for literature; set formats for letters, reports, essays, mails, memos, PPTs.</p>\n<p><strong>Bio-notes</strong> (10.4): short (50 to 200 words), no dates or places, what sort of person you are and how you are different, written objectively. Mission or vision statements are optional; if used, concrete, not clichés. Remove redundancy and padding; use accurate words (button and hole). Budget your words; tell stories, not lists. Baagmati: 278 words/25 sentences → 155/13.</p>\n<p><strong>Describing a photograph</strong> (10.5): no more than 150 words (about 145 to 155). Look at everything first; describe only what is in the picture. Facts, not attitudes: no clichés (<em>fish in cans</em>), no vague <em>lots</em>, no subjective <em>very beautiful</em>, no jokes. Accurate words and order (<em>a ladies’ second class compartment</em>; passive <em>has been provided with</em>). 192 words/13 sentences → 146/14.</p>\n<p><strong>Public purposes</strong> (10.6): no fixed reader. Be formal, factual, precise, brief. Civic problems are man-made. Make notes, gather information (statistical, anecdotal, local, global, types, quantities), describe the harm, suggest remedies. No redundancy (<em>not open because it is closed</em>), no vague phrases (<em>traffic hazard</em>), accurate words (<em>drivers</em>, not <em>motor vehicles</em>). 291 words, little information → 148 words, lots of information.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Correct each sentence and name the rule from Lecture 10.1. (a) Can you give me some informations about the hostel? (b) We bought two breads and some furnitures. (c) She is seeming tired today. (d) I putted the keys on the table last night. (The sentences are mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) ✓ <em>some <strong>information</strong></em>. <em>Information</em> is uncountable: no <em>-s</em>, citation form only.</li>\n<li>(b) ✓ <em>We bought <strong>two loaves of bread</strong> and <strong>some furniture</strong></em> (or <em>two pieces of furniture</em>). Uncountable nouns take no number and no <em>-s</em>; show the amount with a partitive.</li>\n<li>(c) ✓ <em>She <strong>seems</strong> tired today.</em> <em>Seem</em> is not used with <em>-ing</em>.</li>\n<li>(d) ✓ <em>I <strong>put</strong> the keys on the table last night.</em> <em>Put</em> has the same form in all tenses.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Choose the verb by finding the head. (a) The history teachers of our school (is / are) strict. (b) Only one of my cousins (lives / live) in Pune. (c) The Head of Department (has / have) called a meeting. (d) The roses from Bangalore (smells / smell) sweet. (The sentences are mine; the phrases in (c) and (d) are the lecture’s.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Noun + noun: <em>history <strong>teachers</strong></em> → the second noun is the head; <em>teachers</em> is plural → <strong>are</strong>. (<em>Of our school</em> does not change it.)</li>\n<li>(b) The head is <strong>one</strong> (only one cousin) → singular → <strong>lives</strong>, even though <em>cousins</em> is next to the verb.</li>\n<li>(c) Noun + preposition + noun: <em><strong>Head</strong> of Department</em> → the noun before <em>of</em> is the head; one head → <strong>has</strong>.</li>\n<li>(d) <em><strong>Roses</strong> from Bangalore</em> → the noun before <em>from</em> is the head; plural → <strong>smell</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">A friend’s bio-note begins: “My name is Ravi Kumar. My name means the sun. I want a great career in management.” Using Lecture 10.4, what would you change, and why? (The note is mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>My name is Ravi Kumar</em> → <em><strong>I am Ravi Kumar</strong></em>: fewer words, like <em>I am Baagmati</em>.</li>\n<li>Drop <em>My name means the sun</em>: the meaning of a name is <strong>redundant</strong>; a name only identifies a person.</li>\n<li><em>A great career in management</em> is a <strong>cliché</strong>: what is <em>great</em>, what is <em>management</em>? Replace it with a concrete aim, for example <em>In the next five years I want to become a middle-level manager</em>.</li>\n<li>Then use the words saved for what makes Ravi <strong>different</strong>: an unusual childhood, a failure he overcame, a favourite subject and why.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">The first draft of the photo description says: “It seems lots of ladies and children are packed in it like fish in cans.” What is wrong, and what did the final draft say instead?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>Like fish in cans</em> is a <strong>cliché</strong>, and not even true: all seats are taken and some people stand, but nobody is pushing anyone.</li>\n<li><em>Lots of</em> is vague: it “only indicates your inability to count”. Count, or say <em>many</em>, <em>quite a few</em>.</li>\n<li><em>It seems</em> adds nothing definite.</li>\n<li>The final draft gives facts: <em>All seats here are occupied. Some passengers are also standing. … There seem to be above 50 passengers in the compartment.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which of the four demands of public writing (formal, factual, precise, brief) does each sentence break? (a) “Hi Chairman, hope you’re doing great!” (b) “The road near my house is the ugliest, most disgusting road in the whole world.” (c) “Motor vehicles passing through this road have no idea of the depth of the water.” (d) “This store is not open today because it is closed.” (Sentences (a) and (b) are mine; (c) and (d) are the lecture’s.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>Formal</strong>: no <em>Hi</em> and no casual chat to an official, even a friendly one. Begin <em>Sir,</em> and <em>I wish to draw your attention to …</em>.</li>\n<li>(b) <strong>Factual</strong>: <em>ugliest, most disgusting</em> is your attitude, and <em>in the whole world</em> is an exaggeration. Give facts: how long the stretch is, how deep the water is, how many vehicles got stuck.</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>Precise</strong> (accurate): vehicles have no ideas; drivers do. ✓ <em>Drivers have no idea of the road under water.</em></li>\n<li>(d) <strong>Brief</strong>: <em>not open</em> and <em>closed</em> say the same thing twice. ✓ <em>This store is closed today by order of the manager.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">A task says “in no more than 150 words”. Your draft has 192 words in 13 sentences. Is it acceptable? What should you do? (The numbers are those of the lecture’s first draft.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>The lecture allows about 5 words either way: from 145 to 155 words.</li>\n<li>Over the top limit: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">192</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">155</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">37</span></span></span></span></span> words, about <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1901em;vertical-align:-0.345em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8451em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">155</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.394em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">37</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.345em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span></span></span><span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0.24</span></span></span></span>,</span></span> a quarter too many. So it is <strong>not</strong> acceptable.</li>\n<li>Cut redundant phrases (<em>presents a view</em>, <em>bringing the ladies to work and taking them back</em>), clichés, jokes and subjective adjectives.</li>\n<li>Break long sentences into short, simple ones. The lecture’s final draft: 146 words in 14 sentences, inside the limit.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n"});
